#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""cc-eligible — may this work item run OFF-BOX, in a cloud VM?

A cloud VM has no `~/.claude`, no local browser, no dev server, no pane registry, no keychain,
and none of this box's 199 unpushed branches. Which work may go off-box was, until this file,
a SENTENCE IN A PLAN — docs/plans/CONCURRENCY_PROGRAM.md §S5:

    "repo-only work ✅ · visual design ❌ (needs the local browser + dev server) ·
     anything about this box ❌ · branch banking ⚠️"

(longer form, with the measurements behind each verdict: docs/research/concurrency-census-2026-08-07.md §5)

A sentence in a plan is ADVISORY, and advisory is behind a diode: nothing reads it at the moment a
claim is taken (memory: conclusion-must-reach-the-enforcing-store). This file is the ONE
implementation of the predicate, shared by every consumer so a report and a gate can never drift:

    cc-backlog claim --venue cloud  → the claim-side refusal      (`check`)
    a human / a desk report         → the census                  (`sweep`)
    a reader asking why             → the reason                  (`explain`)

Modelled on bin/cc-premise, deliberately: same shape (verdict token on line 1, exit 3 iff refused,
fail-OPEN on everything else), same reason (a per-item predicate must run at CONSUMPTION, every
time, or it is a one-time review that goes stale the moment it finishes).

🚨 THE KEYWORD LIST BELOW IS NOT THE DEFINITION OF THE CLASS. It is a list of SPELLINGS that the
class has been observed to use. A denylist enumerates spellings, never the class it is standing in
for (memory: denylist-enumerates-spellings-not-the-class — a `-rf` denylist let 11 of 13 real cases
past while refusing its own fix). Two consequences are designed in rather than hoped for:

  1. `sweep` prints the ELIGIBLE bucket, titles and all, because that is the bucket where a missed
     spelling does damage. The sweep is the instrument for finding what this list does not say; it
     is not a scoreboard for how well the list is doing. Read it, and ADD the spelling you find.
  2. Nothing here is authoritative enough to be worth widening on a hunch. A word added because it
     "sounds local" costs the cloud tap a whole class of real work, and the tap is the entire point.

FAIL-OPEN, BUT ONLY ABOUT READING. The two halves are different and conflating them is how a
classifier becomes a queue-starver:

  · Cannot READ (ledger absent, malformed, unknown id, an exception) ⇒ exit 0 with a verdict that
    NAMES the uncertainty. A classifier that refused on "I could not tell" would block the whole
    cloud tap on a missing file. This is cc-premise's rule verbatim, and cc-backlog's premise gate
    already depends on it.
  · Read fine, and the text names local-only state ⇒ REFUSE (exit 3). Here the asymmetry points the
    other way and it is worth stating, since it looks like a contradiction of the line above: a
    wrong ELIGIBLE puts a worker in a VM that CANNOT do the work at all and cannot tell you so —
    it will improvise something plausible against state it cannot see. A wrong INELIGIBLE leaves
    the item exactly where it is today, claimable locally, losing nothing. Stranding is
    recoverable; a confident worker on invisible state is not.

BRANCH BANKING IS ITS OWN VERDICT, not a sub-case of "box work", because the plan marks it ⚠️ and
not ❌ and that distinction is real: the WORK is ordinary repo work, and only the CORPUS it operates
on (2,304 commits across 199 branches that exist nowhere but this disk) is local. It refuses — a
worker that cannot see the branches cannot bank them — but under a token that says which half is
the problem, so a reader is not sent looking for a browser.

── THE MEASURED ARM: HISTORY REACH (2026-08-11, W1 of docs/plans/CLOUD_BACKLOG_PIPELINE.md) ────
Everything above this line is a SPELLING. This arm is a MEASUREMENT, and it exists because the
plan's §3 names the shallow clone as "the single biggest determinant of cloud-suitability" and a
spelling cannot express it: an item that says `git show 2ac85e49` names no local-only state at
all, reads ELIGIBLE under every list in this file, and fails off-box because the VM's clone is
grafted at 50 commits and cannot see that object
(docs/research/cloud-vm-shallow-clone-blast-radius-2026-08-11.md).

The measurement is one set membership, not a walk:

  horizon = the newest CLOUD_DEPTH commits of the trunk the VM would clone.
  a cited sha is REACHABLE iff it resolves to a commit AND that commit is in the horizon.

The inference that makes this sound is containment, and it is the reason a NON-resolving sha is
NOT a refusal here: THIS clone is full and the VM's is a subset of it, so an object absent from
the full clone is absent from the VM's too — but it is equally absent from the repo, i.e. it is a
DEAD POINTER, which is cc-premise's subject and not a venue question. Refusing on it here would
convict every item that cites a 12-hex BACKLOG ID (the store is full of them) of needing deep
history. So: resolves + outside the horizon ⇒ refuse; does not resolve ⇒ this arm is silent.

🚨 THE SHALLOW GUARD — keyed on the DANGEROUS EFFECT, not on "am I somewhere unusual" (memory:
guard-refusal-fires-on-its-own-harness). The dangerous effect is a venue=cloud label MINTED FROM A
TRUNCATED CLONE: if the measuring repo is itself grafted at 50 commits, then every sha it can
resolve is inside its own 50-commit horizon by construction, the arm answers REACHABLE to
everything, and it is silently over-permissive in exactly the direction that burns quota. So the
oracle reports `shallow` and REFUSES TO CERTIFY, and `cc-venue` will not write a cloud label
without a certification. That is also what makes "a cloud VM must never build the venue rule"
mechanical rather than a rule in a document: a VM's clone is shallow, so the producer abstains
there by measurement.

The GATE stays fail-open on every unmeasurable state (`no-repo`, `shallow`, `no-horizon`) — a
claim must never be starved by an instrument outage, which is this file's founding rule. The
PRODUCER fails closed on the same states. Those are not in tension: the gate can only ever REFUSE
work that is already claimable locally, while the producer is the thing that PROMOTES work into a
venue where a wrong answer cannot be seen.

Verbs:
  check <id>       `verdict=<token>` on line 1, reasons on stderr. exit 3 iff INELIGIBLE.
  explain <id>     the human-readable reason, on stdout. always exit 0.
  why <id> [--json]  the FULL assessment — every class that fired, plus the history certification.
                   `check` reports ONE token because its only channel is `head -1`; a producer
                   needs all of them, and needs them structured. always exit 0.
  sweep [--json]   the census over every non-done item — one implementation, two consumers.

Store: JSONL at ${CC_BACKLOG_FILE:-~/.claude/autonomy/backlog.jsonl} — the same resolution
cc-backlog and cc-premise use, so a fixtured test and the live desk read the same way.
Repo:  ${CC_ELIGIBLE_REPO} → else $HOME/Development/<project>, which is cc-dispatch's own
       project_repo convention — so the venue arm and the worktree provisioner can never disagree
       about which tree an item's project means.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
from collections import Counter, defaultdict

BACKLOG = os.environ.get(
    "CC_BACKLOG_FILE", os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".claude/autonomy/backlog.jsonl")
)

# ── THE SPELLINGS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read the header before touching these. Each entry is (token, pattern); the token is what `sweep`
# counts and what `explain` names, so a spelling that never fires is visible as a zero rather than
# as a comfortable silence.
#
# Word-boundaried on purpose: `\bcss\b` must not fire inside "success", and `\bpanes?\b` must not
# fire inside "japanese". A substring list here would refuse most of the store.

BOX = [
    # ANYTHING ABOUT THIS BOX — the plan's ❌, and the widest of the three. These name state that
    # exists only in this kernel, this filesystem, this keychain, or this window server. A VM has
    # none of it and, worse, has a plausible-looking empty version of most of it.
    ("launchd", r"\blaunchd\b"),
    ("plist", r"\bplists?\b"),
    ("daemon", r"\bdaemons?\b"),
    ("pane", r"\bpanes?\b"),
    ("terminal", r"\bterminals?\b"),
    ("iterm2", r"\biterm ?2?\b"),
    ("kitty", r"\bkitty\b"),
    ("tmux", r"\btmux\b"),
    # `hook` is the ambiguous one and it is included KNOWINGLY. A hook script is ordinary repo text
    # that a VM can edit perfectly well — but every hook in this repo is specified against the live
    # ~/.claude layer it fires from, and its verification is a Stop/PreToolUse event on THIS box.
    # An off-box worker can write one and can never learn whether it fires. Refusing costs the tap
    # a real class of work; the sweep counts this token separately so that cost stays visible.
    ("hook", r"\bhooks?\b"),
    ("quota", r"\bquotas?\b"),
    ("account", r"\baccounts?\b"),
    ("keychain", r"\bkeychain\b"),
    ("hardware", r"\bhardware\b"),
    ("this-machine", r"\bthis machine\b"),
    ("this-box", r"\bthis box\b"),
    ("reboot", r"\breboots?\b"),
    # THE LIVE LAYER — added 2026-08-11 (W1). The plan's §3 states it outright: `~/.claude` DOES
    # NOT EXIST on the VM. It is the enforcing store this whole repo deploys INTO — a farm of
    # per-file symlinks into a checkout on this disk — so "converge it", "it is stale", "delete
    # the spent scripts from it" is box work in the strictest sense: the artifact under discussion
    # is not in the repo at all. Measured before adding, as this file's header requires: 77 of 485
    # non-done items name it, 25-ish of them in the ELIGIBLE bucket, i.e. this is the largest
    # single class the spelling list was missing.
    ("live-layer", r"\blive layer\b"),
    ("dot-claude", r"~/\.claude\b|\$HOME/\.claude\b"),
    ("deploy-live", r"\bdeploy-live\b"),
    # THE LEDGER ITSELF — added 2026-08-26 (backlog 37b112d8950d). The store is
    # `~/.claude/autonomy/backlog.jsonl`: it lives under the live layer above, is never in the repo,
    # and a VM's copy is the plausible-looking empty version this list's header warns about. The
    # `dot-claude` entry one line up already claims this artifact — but only when an item SPELLS the
    # path, and items about the ledger's CONTENTS never do. They say "457 unvalidated live backlog
    # rows", so they read as ordinary repo work and are promoted.
    #
    # MEASURED, NOT SUPPOSED — this entry exists because the miss was paid for. Item 37b112d8950d
    # ("run the falsifier sweep across all 457 unvalidated live backlog rows, then close-falsified")
    # spells no word in any list above, was dispatched off-box, and the worker found `cc-backlog
    # list --all --json` returning `[]` against a store that does not exist there. One burned worker
    # slot, arriving through the same door the `flyctl` entry below was added to close.
    #
    # NARROW ON PURPOSE, AND THE WIDTH WAS MEASURED — the tempting spelling is `\bcc-backlog\b` and
    # it is exactly the hunch this file's header forbids. Over this repo's own corpus it matches
    # 1,900 lines, worse than the `\bcredentials?\b` (506) that was tested and rejected above,
    # because cc-backlog is the verb EVERY generator calls to file a row: "file a row when the gate
    # fails" is ordinary repo work a VM does perfectly well. The safe unit is the LEDGER-WIDE
    # OPERATION and the STORE ARTIFACT, never the tool — the same choice the entries below make in
    # naming `flyctl` rather than "creds". Corpus hits: falsifier-sweep 1 · live-backlog 5 ·
    # backlog-ledger 7 · cc-backlog-freshness 11 · backlog-store 16 · close-falsified 19 ·
    # cc-premise-sweep 24 · backlog.jsonl 135 — all in the band the accepted `fly-artifact` (14) and
    # `fly deploy` (15) already sit in, and every backlog.jsonl hit genuinely names the store.
    ("backlog-store", r"\bbacklog\.jsonl\b|\bbacklog (?:ledger|store)\b|\blive backlog\b"),
    ("backlog-census", r"\bcc-backlog freshness\b|\bcc-premise (?:sweep|coverage)\b"),
    ("falsifier-sweep", r"\bfalsifier sweep\b|\bclose-falsified\b"),
    # ── FOUND BY READING THE PROMOTED BUCKET (2026-08-11, W1) ──────────────────────────────────
    # Every entry below came out of auditing what `cc-venue run` actually proposed to send, which
    # is this file saying its own instrument works: the ELIGIBLE bucket is where a missed spelling
    # does damage, and a producer made that bucket finally worth reading. None was added on a hunch;
    # each is followed by the item that exposed it.
    #
    # THIS KERNEL AND ITS PROCESS TABLE. BOX already claims "state that exists only in this kernel"
    # as its scope; these are the words that scope is actually written in. A Linux microVM cannot
    # deliver, observe or reason about a Darwin signal, a jetsam band or a sysctl, and — the silent
    # half — it will happily write code that looks like it does.
    ("signal", r"\bsig(?:term|kill|cont|stop|hup)\b"),   # b38279c10c55, 477f0b771ec3
    ("jetsam", r"\bjetsam\b"),                            # 7b762bcbbe11
    ("macos", r"\bmac ?os\b|\bdarwin\b"),                 # 7b762bcbbe11 (memorystatus_control)
    ("sysctl", r"\bsysctl\b"),                            # 6a82c9405b9e
    # A LIVE TICK OF A LOCAL DAEMON. The item that exposed this says it outright — its logic is
    # already proven by bats and what is unproven is "the deployed reach", which is a claim about
    # this box that no test in a VM can settle.
    ("live-tick", r"\blive tick\b"),                      # 9f69e13fc2e0
    ("runner-log", r"\brunner\.log\b"),                   # 9f69e13fc2e0
    # THE SESSION MACHINERY. Same rationale the `hook` entry above is included under, verbatim: a
    # VM can write the change and can never learn whether it fires. Spawning, a teammate inheriting
    # a cwd, a self-close, and the Bash-tool text layer are all events of a LIVE Claude Code
    # session, and this repo has exactly one machine that has them.
    ("spawn", r"\bspawn(?:s|ed|ing)?\b"),                 # e43e95c0ad18
    ("teammate", r"\bteammates?\b|\bassignees?\b"),       # d4fa449e3895
    ("self-close", r"\bself-close\b"),                    # d4fa449e3895
    ("tool-layer", r"\bbash[- ]tool\b|\bzsh layer\b"),    # 6bfd83f03c3a, 1d20ff5ee344
    ("claude1", r"\bclaude1\b"),                          # 492b95cbac72, 48e14163e78a
    ("claude-exe", r"\bclaude\.exe\b"),                   # e78107996dea (a process on this box)
    ("start-latency", r"\bstart[- ]latency\b"),           # 492b95cbac72
    # ── FOUND BY THE LEAD'S BLIND-ADJUDICATION DIFF (2026-08-11, the first standing audit) ─────
    # W1's close named auditing the promoted bucket a STANDING duty, not a one-time pass. The
    # first post-land audit diffed the lead's 27-item blind triage (written before this producer
    # existed) against the labels; the two disagreements were both cloud labels, and both hid box
    # state in spellings this list lacked. Measured before adding: the whole cloud bucket (48)
    # exposes exactly these two items and no others; `transcript`/`telemetry` were candidates and
    # stayed OUT (zero cloud instances — no hunch entries). Both items' texts are pinned in the
    # suite.
    #
    # THE INSTALLED BINARY FAMILY. `.claude-219`, `.claude-220`, `.claude-next`, the knowledge
    # mirrors — versioned config/install dirs that exist only on this disk; and any claim about
    # what an INSTALLED binary contains ("asyncRewake ABSENT from installed … binaries") is a
    # read of this box, not of the repo. The version-string spelling is the same family from the
    # other side: adjudicating 2.1.NNN build behavior ("2.1.220 IS 3.4x worse per session") is a
    # statement about builds installed and measured HERE — a VM has neither the builds nor the
    # sessions.
    ("versioned-config-dir", r"\.claude-(?:\d+|next\d*|secondary|tertiary)\b"),  # 7d8e44dd5822
    ("installed-binaries", r"\binstalled\b.{0,40}\bbinar(?:y|ies)\b|\bbinar(?:y|ies)\b.{0,20}\binstalled\b"),  # 7d8e44dd5822
    ("cc-binary-version", r"\b2\.1\.\d{3}\b"),            # bbad96d163ab
]

OFFBOX_LANE = [
    # ── THE LANE CANNOT MODIFY ITSELF ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # A class the plan HAD to have and nothing here expressed, found the only way it could be — by
    # reading what the producer proposed. `7c6ff16259a0` is the brief CLOUD_BACKLOG_PIPELINE.md §2
    # names as the one REJECTED OUTRIGHT by hand triage, and it sat in the first cloud bucket this
    # producer ever printed. It is invisible to every list above: its span never says handoff-fire,
    # never says pane, never says launchd. It says "off-box payload", "cc-cloud preflight",
    # "unblock the cloud lane".
    #
    # The rule is the same circularity the plan applies to W1 itself ("a cloud VM building its own
    # admission rule is a circular dependency"), generalised one step: A SESSION THIS LANE CREATED
    # CANNOT VERIFY A CHANGE TO THE LANE. If the change is wrong, the failure mode is that the fire
    # does not happen, or happens into an invented branch, or pushes nothing — and every one of
    # those is invisible from inside the session that the broken lane was supposed to produce. It
    # is not a statement about difficulty; it is that the observer and the subject are the same
    # object.
    #
    # `venue` is in the list for the sharpest case of all: it would refuse an item asking to edit
    # THIS FILE. `e15a743e12ba` ("shrink the off-box exclusion list with a census run") is exactly
    # that item, and it was in the same bucket.
    ("off-box", r"\boff[- ]?box\b"),
    ("cloud-lane", r"\bcloud lane\b"),
    ("cc-cloud", r"\bcc-cloud\b"),
    ("cc-offload", r"\bcc-offload\b"),
    ("cloud-session", r"\bcloud vms?\b|\bcloud sessions?\b"),
    ("venue", r"\bvenue\b"),
]

SPAWN_RAIL = [
    # THE FLEET'S SPAWN RAIL — its own class rather than a member of BOX, because the plan's
    # hand-triage singled it out as the one brief REJECTED OUTRIGHT: `7c6ff16259a0` patches
    # scripts/handoff-fire.sh, "strands real work box-wide if wrong" in its own words
    # (CLOUD_BACKLOG_PIPELINE.md §2). The file is ordinary repo text — a VM can edit it perfectly —
    # and that is exactly the trap: the edit is cheap, the VERIFICATION is a live fire on this box,
    # and a wrong one takes every session on the machine with it. Refusing costs the tap 7 items
    # (measured over the eligible bucket); the alternative cost is the fleet.
    #
    # NARROW ON PURPOSE. `cc-dispatch`, `self-close` and the rest of the dispatch stack are NOT
    # here: they are repo scripts with repo-level tests, and widening a denylist on a hunch is how
    # the tap starves (this file's header, and memory: denylist-enumerates-spellings-not-the-class).
    # Spaced as well as hyphenated: `ce2bf742216d` says "handoff fire queue" and was promoted to
    # cloud by the hyphen-only form — the same subject under the spelling a human types.
    ("handoff-fire", r"\bhandoff[- ]fire(?:\.sh)?\b"),
    ("spawn-rail", r"\bspawn rail\b"),
]

GITHUB = [
    # NEEDS GITHUB BEYOND THE ONE CLONED REPO. §3: "No `gh` CLI. GitHub reaches the VM only through
    # MCP tools scoped to one repository. Anything needing a PR is out." So the class is not "a PR"
    # — it is every reach past the single `git_repository` source the create wired in: a pull
    # request, an org or repo SETTING, the web console, the CLI.
    #
    # THREE OF THESE FIVE FIRE ZERO TIMES over the live store today, and they ship anyway. That is
    # this file's design, not an oversight: `sweep` prints "never fired" for a dead spelling, so a
    # zero is VISIBLE and can be read as either "the class is empty" or "the word is wrong" — a
    # spelling omitted until it is needed is instead a silent hole on the day it is (memory:
    # sensor-default-off-makes-blindness-the-shipping-path). `github` is the one that fires (4).
    #
    # `PR` is the only CASE-SENSITIVE pattern in this file — `(?-i:...)` locally undoes the re.I
    # every pattern here is compiled with. Lowercased, `\bpr\b` is a two-letter token that appears
    # in ordinary prose and in flag fragments, and this list's whole job is to not refuse the store.
    ("pull-request", r"\bpull requests?\b"),
    ("pr", r"(?-i:\bPRs?\b)"),
    ("gh-cli", r"\bgh (?:pr|issue|api|repo|auth|run|workflow)\b|\bgh CLI\b"),
    ("github", r"\bgithub\b"),
    ("github-app", r"\bgithub app\b"),
]

VISUAL = [
    # VISUAL / DESIGN — the plan's other ❌. Every measurement in the originating session ran
    # through the local browser against localhost:3554; a sandboxed clone has neither server nor
    # browser nor eyes. The failure is silent: a worker "verifies" a render it never saw.
    ("screenshot", r"\bscreen ?shots?\b"),
    ("browser", r"\bbrowsers?\b"),
    ("dev-server", r"\bdev server\b"),
    ("localhost", r"\blocalhost\b"),
    ("visual", r"\bvisual(?:ly)?\b"),
    ("css", r"\bcss\b"),
    ("render", r"\brender(?:s|ed|ing)?\b"),
    ("pixel", r"\bpixels?\b"),
    ("banner", r"\bbanners?\b"),
    # The Chrome DevTools Protocol — a browser this box drives, not a library
    # (found in the promoted bucket: e09a075539f5, a CDP consent dialog that re-pops).
    ("cdp", r"\bcdp\b"),
    # A console, a click, a UI: the same failure as `screenshot` — a worker "verifies" an
    # interaction it never had (found in the promoted bucket: f3c1781db2ba, a provisioning
    # console driven by one click). `UI` is case-sensitive for the reason `PR` is: lowercased it
    # is a two-letter token that appears inside ordinary prose.
    ("ui", r"(?-i:\bUI\b)"),
    ("click", r"\bclicks?\b"),
]

BANKING = [
    # BRANCH BANKING — the plan's ⚠️. The work is ordinary; the corpus is not.
    ("branch-banking", r"\bbranch bank(?:ing|ed)?\b"),
    ("worktree", r"\bwork ?trees?\b"),
    ("unpushed", r"\bunpushed\b"),
    ("unlanded", r"\bunlanded\b"),
    ("stranded", r"\bstranded\b"),
    ("local-branch", r"\blocal(?:-only)? branch(?:es)?\b"),
    # THE WORKTREE UNDER THE NAME A HUMAN ACTUALLY TYPES. `worktree` above catches the word; this
    # catches the DIRECTORY, which is how the fleet refers to one in practice — the same argument
    # `handoff-fire` already makes in SPAWN_RAIL, where the spaced form had to be added because
    # `ce2bf742216d` was promoted to cloud by the hyphen-only pattern.
    #
    # MEASURED, because widening a denylist on a hunch is how the tap starves (this file's header).
    # Over every docs/**/*.md line in this repo the pattern matches 198 distinct tokens, and ALL 198
    # are worktree names — `wt-pool-1`, `wt-bsm-gap`, `wt-cc-225106-82355`, `wt-run-57191`. There is
    # no second meaning for this prefix in the corpus, which is what makes a prefix pattern safe
    # here when a bare-count one is not: `\d+ commits` was tested alongside it and REJECTED at 309
    # hits, because "800 commits behind origin/main" is staleness prose, not a corpus on this disk.
    #
    # THE MISS THIS CLOSES, and it is not hypothetical. Backlog `4abcbbbbc997` ("Retry the BSM land
    # on a QUIET machine … 7 commits on research/bsm-world-class-gap (wt-bsm-gap), tree clean")
    # names its worktree ONLY as `wt-bsm-gap`, spells no other listed word, and so was classified
    # `eligible` and dispatched to a cloud VM — where the reso-management-app repo, the branch, the
    # worktree and the backlog store all do not exist, so the land it asks for is unreachable by
    # construction. One burned worker slot per dispatch. Respell the same title with the word
    # `worktree` and the pre-existing list already refuses it; that near-miss is the whole defect.
    ("wt-slug", r"\bwt-[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*\b"),
]

EXTERNAL_DEPLOY = [
    # DEPLOY TO EXTERNAL INFRA — a THIRD axis, and the reason it is not folded into BOX or GITHUB.
    # BOX names state that exists only on this disk; GITHUB names reach past the one cloned repo.
    # This names neither: the artifact is ordinary repo text (a `vector.toml`, a `fly.toml`), the
    # repo is the item's own, and the work still cannot finish off-box because FINISHING IT MEANS
    # DEPLOYING — a provider CLI the VM does not install and a credential it does not hold. Same
    # structure as the `hook` entry's argument: a VM can write the change perfectly and can never
    # learn whether it took.
    #
    # THE MISS THIS CLOSES, measured not supposed. Backlog `96c57c1c4a6c` — the fly-log-shipper-iad
    # filter that drops a serving app's logs — says `flyctl apps create`, says the directory "has
    # NEVER been deployed", and spells no word in any list above. `cc-eligible why` on its real
    # title returns `verdict=eligible / refused: (nothing fired)`, so it is dispatched to a VM that
    # has no flyctl, no Fly credential, and (its project being reso-management-app) not even the
    # repo. One burned worker slot per dispatch — the same cost the `wt-slug` entry above was added
    # to stop, arriving through a door that entry does not cover.
    #
    # NARROW ON PURPOSE, AND THE WIDTH WAS MEASURED. The tempting spelling is the ABSTRACT one —
    # the item does say "creds" — and it is precisely the hunch this file's header forbids: over
    # this repo's own corpus `\bcredentials?\b` matches 506 lines and `\bsecrets?\b` 133, so either
    # would refuse a large slice of ordinary repo work and starve the tap outright. The PROVIDER is
    # the safe unit, exactly as the lists above name `launchd` and `jetsam` rather than "OS state":
    # `flyctl` matches 3 lines, the Fly artifact suffixes 14, `fly deploy` 15 — all of them real
    # Fly.io infrastructure, no second meaning in the corpus. Bare `\bfly\b` was TESTED AND
    # REJECTED: it takes "flying" with it, which is the word-boundary failure this suite pairs a
    # lookalike control against.
    #
    # Refusing is the safe side under this file's own asymmetry rule: a wrong INELIGIBLE leaves the
    # item claimable locally, losing nothing, while a wrong ELIGIBLE puts a worker in a VM that
    # cannot do the work and cannot say so.
    ("flyctl", r"\bflyctl\b"),                       # 96c57c1c4a6c
    ("fly-artifact", r"\bfly\.(?:io|toml|dev)\b"),   # 96c57c1c4a6c (sibling shipper dirs)
    ("fly-deploy", r"\bfly deploy\b"),
]

# Compiled once, in REFUSAL ORDER. Order decides which verdict a multi-class item reports, and it
# runs widest-first: an item that says both "launchd" and "render" is box work whose remedy happens
# to be visible, not design work. The alternative — reporting every class — makes the verdict token
# unparseable by a `head -1` consumer, which is the only channel cc-dispatch has.
#
# THE TWO SELF-REFERENTIAL CLASSES COME FIRST, ahead of the widest one, because they are the only
# entries whose wrong answer is not confined to the item. A bad edit to the spawn rail strands every
# session on the box; a bad edit to the off-box lane strands the mechanism that would have reported
# it. So when an item is both lane work and box work, the reader must be sent to the lane.
CLASSES = [
    ("ineligible-offbox-lane", "THE OFF-BOX LANE ITSELF — the lane cannot verify a change to itself", OFFBOX_LANE),
    ("ineligible-spawn-rail", "THE FLEET'S SPAWN RAIL — verified only by a live fire on this box", SPAWN_RAIL),
    ("ineligible-box", "anything about THIS BOX — local-only state a VM cannot see", BOX),
    ("ineligible-visual", "VISUAL/DESIGN — needs the local browser + dev server", VISUAL),
    ("ineligible-branch-banking", "BRANCH BANKING — the corpus exists only on this disk", BANKING),
    # THE TWO REACH-PAST-THE-VM CLASSES, last and adjacent because they answer the same question
    # from two sides: what does finishing this item touch that the VM's single clone does not
    # contain? External deploy leads GitHub only because a Fly item that also says "PR" is deploy
    # work whose landing happens to need a PR, not the other way round.
    ("ineligible-external-deploy", "DEPLOY TO EXTERNAL INFRA — needs a provider CLI + credential the VM has none of", EXTERNAL_DEPLOY),
    ("ineligible-github", "NEEDS GITHUB beyond the one cloned repo — no gh CLI off-box", GITHUB),
]
CLASSES = [(v, d, [(t, re.compile(p, re.I)) for t, p in pats]) for v, d, pats in CLASSES]

# THE MEASURED CLASS. Not in CLASSES because CLASSES is the spelling table and `classify()` over it
# is PURE — no I/O, no git, no ledger — which is what makes this file's fail-open rule enforceable
# in one place. This verdict is produced by `assess()`, which owns the arm that can fail.
#
# LAST in refusal order, deliberately. Every entry above it names a WORD a human can read in the
# title and act on; this one names a property of the graph. When an item is both, the spelling is
# the more actionable sentence to lead with — and `why` reports both regardless.
DEEP_HISTORY = "ineligible-deep-history"

# ── THE SECOND MEASURED CLASS: THE ITEM'S REPO IS NOT THE REPO THE VM IS GIVEN ─────────────────
# MEASURED 2026-08-23 over the whole live cloud population, and it is not one contributor among
# many — it is the DOMINANT cause of the 133-session `NOT-STARTED` pile. Joining each declaration's
# `item=` to that item's `project` in the backlog puts 106 of 133 (80%) on a repo the VM was never
# given: 92 reso-management-app, 14 doc_classifier, against 13 for claude-infrastructure.
#
# THOSE SESSIONS DID NOT FAIL TO START — THE LABEL IS A MISNOMER FOR THEM. 130 of the 133 still
# answer `GET /v1/code/sessions/<id>`, and 119 read `environment_kind: anthropic_cloud` AND
# `sources: 1` — a real VM with a repository attached, i.e. fully able to push. They booted, read
# the brief, found the project absent and correctly stopped, in their own words:
#   "reso-management-app repo absent in cloud VM; backlog item unreachable"
#   "wrong venue: reso-management-app not in this container"
#   "doc_classifier repo not accessible; item requires on-box dispatch"
# One sampled session spent $2.00 and 15,464 output tokens to reach that verdict. `cc-cloud` calls
# every one of them NOT-STARTED because its only sensor is whether the declared branch appeared,
# and a session that correctly declines to do the work commits nothing to push.
#
# WHY THE SPELLING TABLE ABOVE COULD NOT CATCH THIS, AND WHY WIDENING IT NEVER WILL. Every class in
# CLASSES is a regex over the item's TEXT, so it can only refuse an item that happens to SAY a
# refused word. Reachability is not a word — it is a property of the pair (the item's project, the
# one repo `cc-offload` attaches). BANKING's own `wt-slug` entry is this same defect caught one item
# at a time: `4abcbbbbc997` was promoted to cloud purely because it spelled its worktree `wt-bsm-gap`
# and never the word `worktree`. Two of the burned sessions named the gap outright — "root cause:
# cc-eligible lacks cross-repo arm" and "cc-eligible gate doesn't check project scope".
#
# BEFORE the history arm in refusal order: if the repo is not there at all, the depth of its clone
# is not the sentence to lead with, and every sha in the item is unreachable for a reason that has
# nothing to do with a horizon.
CROSS_REPO = "ineligible-cross-repo"

# Every refusing verdict with its one-line description, spelling-backed and measured alike. The
# census renders from THIS, not from CLASSES, so a measured class cannot be invisible in the report
# that exists to find what the classifier misses.
SWEEP_ROWS = [(v, d) for v, d, _p in CLASSES] + [
    (CROSS_REPO, "ANOTHER REPO — the VM is given exactly one, and this is not it (measured)"),
    (DEEP_HISTORY, "NEEDS HISTORY the shallow cloud clone cannot see (measured)"),
]

# The verdicts that REFUSE (exit 3). One tuple so the gate, the census and the tests can never
# disagree about which is which — cc-premise's BLOCKING, same reason.
BLOCKING = tuple(v for v, _d, _p in CLASSES) + (CROSS_REPO, DEEP_HISTORY)

# The uncertainty verdicts. Both exit 0. They are SEPARATE tokens because they mean different
# things to whoever reads the census: "no such item" is a caller bug, "unreadable store" is an
# outage, and a single `unknown` for both would hide an outage inside a typo (memory:
# sensor-default-off-makes-blindness-the-shipping-path).
UNKNOWN_ITEM = "unknown-item"
UNKNOWN_STORE = "unknown-store"


def _open_unknown(token, msg):
    """Any failure to READ: say so on stderr, name the uncertainty on line 1, exit 0."""
    sys.stderr.write("cc-eligible: %s\n" % msg)
    sys.stdout.write("verdict=%s\n" % token)
    sys.exit(0)


def load_records():
    """Every well-formed ledger record, in file order. Malformed lines are skipped, never fatal.

    Deliberately NOT a status fold and deliberately NOT a call back into cc-backlog: `check` runs
    INSIDE cc-backlog's claim path, so shelling out to `cc-backlog list` would re-enter the subject
    on its own hot path. `sweep` — which is not on that path — asks cc-backlog for the fold instead,
    so there stays exactly one implementation of "what status is this item".
    """
    recs = []
    try:
        with open(BACKLOG, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    obj = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue  # malformed: cc-backlog reports these; we must not add a second voice
                if isinstance(obj, dict) and obj.get("id"):
                    recs.append(obj)
    except (IOError, OSError) as exc:
        _open_unknown(UNKNOWN_STORE, "ledger unreadable (%s) — fail-open" % exc)
    return recs


# THE CLASSIFIED SPAN is the item's SPECIFICATION, not everything ever written under its id
# (memory: assertion-span-must-equal-its-subject). `title`, `dodRef`, `condition` and `source` say
# what the work IS. `evidence` is the proof a finished item left behind, and `needs` is an
# operator-only step — an item carrying one is `blocked`, i.e. outside every dispatch wave, so
# classifying on it could only ever add false refusals to work that is never claimed anyway.
SPAN_FIELDS = ("title", "dodRef", "condition", "source")


def build_index(recs):
    """id -> the item's specification text, accumulated across its records.

    Accumulated rather than last-write-wins because these fields are written by the `add` record and
    an item may be re-added (the id is a hash of project+title+source, so a re-add is idempotent and
    carries the same words) — taking the union costs nothing and cannot lose a field that only one
    record carried.
    """
    idx = {}
    for r in recs:
        rid = r["id"]
        ent = idx.setdefault(rid, {"text": "", "title": "", "project": ""})
        parts = [r.get(k) or "" for k in SPAN_FIELDS]
        text = " ".join(p for p in parts if p)
        if text:
            ent["text"] = (ent["text"] + " " + text).strip()
        if r.get("title") and not ent["title"]:
            ent["title"] = r["title"]
        if r.get("project") and not ent["project"]:
            ent["project"] = r["project"]
    return idx


def classify_all(text):
    """[{verdict, description, tokens}] for EVERY spelling class that fired, in refusal order.

    Pure — no I/O, no ledger, no git. Everything that can fail lives in the callers, which is what
    makes the fail-open rule enforceable in one place. The primitive, so that `classify` (one
    token, for the gate) and `assess_full` (all of them, for the producer) cannot drift into two
    readings of the same table.
    """
    out = []
    for verdict, desc, pats in CLASSES:
        hits = [tok for tok, rx in pats if rx.search(text)]
        if hits:
            out.append({"verdict": verdict, "description": desc, "tokens": hits})
    return out


def classify(text):
    """(verdict, description, [matched tokens]) — the FIRST class that fires, or eligible."""
    hits = classify_all(text)
    if hits:
        return hits[0]["verdict"], hits[0]["description"], hits[0]["tokens"]
    return "eligible", "repo-only work — no local-only state named", []


# ── THE HISTORY HORIZON ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 50 is the MEASURED depth of a real cloud clone, not a policy choice
# (cloud-vm-shallow-clone-blast-radius-2026-08-11.md §1: `git rev-list --count HEAD` → 50,
# `--is-shallow-repository` → true). Overridable so a test can pin a tiny horizon and so a change
# on the provisioning side has one place to land.
CLOUD_DEPTH = 50
try:
    CLOUD_DEPTH = int(os.environ.get("CC_ELIGIBLE_HISTORY_DEPTH") or CLOUD_DEPTH)
except ValueError:
    pass

# 7 is git's floor for an abbreviation here (2034 objects on the VM ⇒ `%h` yields 7), 40 is a full
# sha. Word-boundaried and lowercase-only: an upper-case hex run is not how this repo writes a sha,
# and `[0-9a-f]{7,40}` unanchored would match inside longer identifiers.
HEX = re.compile(r"\b[0-9a-f]{7,40}\b")

GIT_TIMEOUT_S = 20


class HistoryOracle:
    """Can a cloud clone of <repo> SEE the commits this item cites?

    One instance per repo per process. Everything is lazy and memoised, because `check` runs inside
    cc-backlog's claim path and must not pay for a census it does not need.

    `state` is the certification and it has four values, three of which are "I could not tell":
      ok          — a full clone, a resolvable trunk, a horizon of CLOUD_DEPTH commits.
      no-repo     — no directory, or not a git repo, or git is unusable.
      shallow     — THE GUARD. This clone is itself grafted, so its horizon is its whole visible
                    history and every resolvable sha would read REACHABLE. It cannot certify.
      no-horizon  — a repo, unshallow, but neither origin/<trunk> nor HEAD resolves.
    """

    def __init__(self, repo, depth=None):
        self.repo = repo
        self.depth = CLOUD_DEPTH if depth is None else depth
        self.state = None
        self.ref = ""
        self.horizon = set()
        self._resolved = {}

    def _git(self, args):
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(
                ["git", "-C", self.repo] + args,
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=GIT_TIMEOUT_S,
            )
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            return None
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return p.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace")

    def certify(self):
        """state, computed once. Never raises — an oracle that throws would reach the claim path."""
        if self.state is not None:
            return self.state
        if not self.repo or not os.path.isdir(self.repo):
            self.state = "no-repo"
            return self.state
        if self._git(["rev-parse", "--git-dir"]) is None:
            self.state = "no-repo"
            return self.state
        # THE GUARD, before the horizon: a shallow clone answers `rev-list -n 50` perfectly well.
        shallow = self._git(["rev-parse", "--is-shallow-repository"])
        if shallow is not None and shallow.strip() == "true":
            self.state = "shallow"
            return self.state
        for ref in ("origin/main", "origin/master", "HEAD"):
            out = self._git(["rev-list", "-n", str(self.depth), ref])
            if out and out.split():
                self.ref, self.horizon = ref, set(out.split())
                self.state = "ok"
                return self.state
        self.state = "no-horizon"
        return self.state

    def resolve_many(self, tokens):
        """Populate the token→sha cache in ONE `cat-file --batch-check`.

        Called with the whole store's tokens by `sweep` and with one item's by `check`, so the
        process count is bounded by the number of CALLERS rather than by the number of shas.

        Output is matched to input BY ORDER, never by parsing the echoed line: git echoes the
        original request verbatim for `missing`/`ambiguous` (i.e. WITH the `^{commit}` suffix) and
        the resolved sha for a hit, so the two shapes share no field to key on.
        """
        want = [t for t in tokens if t not in self._resolved]
        if not want:
            return
        want = sorted(set(want))
        try:
            p = subprocess.run(
                ["git", "-C", self.repo, "cat-file", "--batch-check"],
                input="".join(t + "^{commit}\n" for t in want).encode(),
                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=GIT_TIMEOUT_S,
            )
            lines = p.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines()
        except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
            lines = []
        for tok, line in zip(want, lines):
            parts = line.split()
            if len(parts) >= 2 and parts[1] == "commit":
                self._resolved[tok] = parts[0]          # a real commit
            elif len(parts) >= 2 and parts[-1] == "ambiguous":
                self._resolved[tok] = "ambiguous"
            else:
                self._resolved[tok] = None              # not an object here: a backlog id, or dead
        for tok in want:                                # a short read must not re-shell every call
            self._resolved.setdefault(tok, None)

    def unreachable(self, text):
        """[(token, why)] for every cited sha a cloud clone of this repo could NOT see.

        Empty on every uncertified state — silence, not a false all-clear: `certify()` is what a
        caller reads to tell "nothing to refuse" from "I could not look".
        """
        if self.certify() != "ok":
            return []
        toks = HEX.findall(text)
        if not toks:
            return []
        self.resolve_many(toks)
        out, seen = [], set()
        for t in toks:
            if t in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(t)
            sha = self._resolved.get(t)
            if sha is None:
                continue                                # not a commit here ⇒ not this arm's subject
            if sha == "ambiguous":
                # An abbreviation that matches more than one object cannot be certified either way,
                # and §4 of the blast-radius doc names exactly this: a 7-char sha minted on a VM is
                # a lookup key that may not survive resolution against a full clone. Conservative,
                # because the asymmetry in this file's header points here — stranding is
                # recoverable, a confident worker on invisible history is not.
                out.append((t, "ambiguous abbreviation — cannot certify reach"))
            elif sha not in self.horizon:
                out.append((t, "resolves to %s, outside the newest %d commits of %s"
                            % (sha[:12], self.depth, self.ref)))
        return out


def _git_out(repo, args):
    """One git read against <repo>, or None. NEVER raises: an unmeasurable state must fail OPEN,
    and this arm is reached from inside cc-backlog's claim path."""
    if not repo or not os.path.isdir(repo):
        return None
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            ["git", "-C", repo] + args,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            timeout=GIT_TIMEOUT_S, text=True,
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return p.stdout.strip() if p.returncode == 0 else None


def _norm_remote(url):
    """`git@host:owner/name.git`, `https://host/owner/name` and `ssh://host/owner/name/` all mean
    owner/name. Compared normalised so two spellings of ONE repo are never read as two repos —
    the false-positive direction, which would refuse work that is in fact reachable."""
    if not url:
        return ""
    u = url.strip().rstrip("/")
    if u.endswith(".git"):
        u = u[:-4]
    u = re.sub(r"^[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9+.-]*://", "", u)   # scheme
    u = re.sub(r"^[^@/]+@", "", u)                      # user@
    u = u.replace(":", "/")                             # scp-style host:owner/name
    parts = [p for p in u.split("/") if p]
    return "/".join(parts[-2:]).lower() if len(parts) >= 2 else u.lower()


def _origin_of(repo):
    return _git_out(repo, ["remote", "get-url", "origin"])


# ── THE ONE REPO A CLOUD VM IS GIVEN ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `cc-offload`'s API create attaches exactly ONE `git_repository` source, derived from the origin of
# its own `$REPO` — `bin/cc-offload:84`, `REPO="${CC_OFFLOAD_REPO:-$ROOT}"`, ROOT being the install
# root of the off-box lane's own tooling, i.e. the repo THIS FILE lives in.
#
# THE LANE IS A REPO, SO IT IS IDENTIFIED BY ITS ORIGIN AND FOUND VIA THIS SCRIPT — never by
# assembling $HOME/Development/<name>. That assembly was tried first and is wrong in exactly the
# place this work happens: inside a git WORKTREE, `rev-parse --show-toplevel` yields the worktree
# directory, so the basename is the BRANCH name (`cloud-notstarted`) and the lane resolved to a path
# that does not exist. Reading the origin of whatever repo contains this file is right in a worktree
# and in the shared checkout alike, because both carry the same origin.
#
# GATED ON LIVING UNDER $HOME/Development, and that guard is load-bearing rather than stylistic.
# All seven suites that reach this predicate fixture $HOME to a temp dir; an ungated __file__
# derivation would measure their temp-dir projects against the operator's REAL checkout and read
# `ineligible-cross-repo` for every fixture — a new blocking arm silently reddening its siblings.
# With the guard, the real script is not under the fixtured $HOME, the lane is unresolvable, and the
# arm fails open, which is this file's law for every state it cannot measure.
#
# `CC_ELIGIBLE_CLOUD_REPO=""` (set, but empty) is the kill switch — spelled as a value rather than a
# second flag, so the override and the off-switch cannot disagree about which repo the lane holds.
_LANE_REPO = []


def cloud_repo():
    """The single repo a cloud VM is given, or "" when it cannot be determined (⇒ arm disabled)."""
    override = os.environ.get("CC_ELIGIBLE_CLOUD_REPO")
    if override is not None:
        return override
    off = os.environ.get("CC_OFFLOAD_REPO")
    if off:
        return off
    if not _LANE_REPO:
        _LANE_REPO.append(_lane_from_self())
    return _LANE_REPO[0]


def _lane_from_self():
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    top = _git_out(here, ["rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if not top:
        return ""
    dev = os.path.realpath(os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), "Development"))
    return top if os.path.realpath(top).startswith(dev + os.sep) else ""


def cross_repo(project):
    """(unreachable, why) — is this item's project outside the ONE repo a cloud VM is given?

    Fails OPEN in every direction it cannot measure, exactly like the history oracle: this arm
    refuses only on a fact it established. The three open cases are a disabled/unresolvable lane, a
    projectless item, and a lane path that is not a readable git repo (which is what a fixtured
    $HOME produces).
    """
    lane = cloud_repo()
    if not lane or not project:
        return False, ""
    lane_origin = _norm_remote(_origin_of(lane))
    if not lane_origin:
        return False, ""                       # lane not a readable git repo ⇒ nothing to measure
    item_repo = repo_for(project)
    if not item_repo:
        return False, ""
    if os.path.realpath(item_repo) == os.path.realpath(lane):
        return False, ""                       # the same tree, reached by another path
    item_origin = _norm_remote(_origin_of(item_repo))
    if item_origin and item_origin == lane_origin:
        return False, ""                       # ONE repo, two checkouts — reachable after all
    seen = item_origin or ("no readable git repo at %s" % item_repo)
    return True, "project %r is %s; the VM is given only %s" % (project, seen, lane_origin)


def repo_for(project):
    """The tree an item's project means. cc-dispatch's project_repo convention, deliberately.

    `CC_ELIGIBLE_REPO` is a single global override and is therefore only honoured as the answer for
    EVERY project — it is used by the tests and by a caller that already knows it holds one repo.
    A projectless item gets "", which certifies `no-repo` and refuses to promote rather than
    silently measuring against whatever tree this process happens to sit in.
    """
    override = os.environ.get("CC_ELIGIBLE_REPO")
    if override:
        return override
    if not project:
        return ""
    return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), "Development", project)


_ORACLES = {}


def oracle_for(project):
    repo = repo_for(project)
    if repo not in _ORACLES:
        _ORACLES[repo] = HistoryOracle(repo)
    return _ORACLES[repo]


def assess(item_id, idx):
    """(verdict, description, tokens, title) — the predicate, over a built index.

    The SPELLING arms run first and short-circuit: they are pure and free, the measured arm shells
    out to git, and an item already refused for naming `launchd` gains nothing from a horizon read.
    `assess_full` is the verb-facing form that runs both regardless.
    """
    full = assess_full(item_id, idx)
    return full["verdict"], full["description"], full["tokens"], full["title"]


def assess_full(item_id, idx, measure=True):
    """The whole assessment, as a dict — every class that fired plus the history certification.

    `check` collapses this to one token because `head -1` is its only channel; `cc-venue` needs all
    of it, and needs it from THIS function rather than from a second implementation, which is the
    whole reason this file exists (one predicate, a gate and a report that cannot drift).
    """
    ent = idx.get(item_id)
    if ent is None:
        return {
            "id": item_id, "verdict": UNKNOWN_ITEM, "description": "no such item in %s" % BACKLOG,
            "tokens": [], "title": "", "project": "", "classes": [],
            "history": {"state": "not-measured", "repo": "", "ref": "", "depth": CLOUD_DEPTH,
                        "unreachable": []},
            "reach": {"state": "not-measured", "lane": "", "why": ""},
        }
    text = ent["text"]
    classes = classify_all(text)

    project = ent.get("project", "")
    orc = oracle_for(project)
    hist = {"state": "not-measured", "repo": orc.repo, "ref": "", "depth": orc.depth,
            "unreachable": []}
    reach = {"state": "not-measured", "lane": "", "why": ""}
    if measure:
        # REACH BEFORE HISTORY, in that order: a repo the VM never receives has no horizon worth
        # reporting, and "this is another repo" is the sentence that tells a reader what to do.
        reach["lane"] = cloud_repo()
        elsewhere, why = cross_repo(project)
        reach["state"] = "elsewhere" if elsewhere else "reachable-or-unmeasured"
        reach["why"] = why
        if elsewhere:
            classes.append({
                "verdict": CROSS_REPO,
                "description": "ANOTHER REPO — the VM is given exactly one and this is not it",
                "tokens": [project],
            })
        hist["state"] = orc.certify()
        hist["ref"] = orc.ref
        bad = orc.unreachable(text)
        hist["unreachable"] = [{"sha": t, "why": w} for t, w in bad]
        if bad:
            classes.append({
                "verdict": DEEP_HISTORY,
                "description": "NEEDS HISTORY the %d-commit cloud clone cannot see" % orc.depth,
                "tokens": [t for t, _w in bad],
            })

    if classes:
        first = classes[0]
        return {
            "id": item_id, "verdict": first["verdict"], "description": first["description"],
            "tokens": first["tokens"], "title": ent["title"], "project": project,
            "classes": classes, "history": hist, "reach": reach,
        }
    return {
        "id": item_id, "verdict": "eligible",
        "description": "repo-only work — no local-only state named", "tokens": [],
        "title": ent["title"], "project": project, "classes": [], "history": hist,
        "reach": reach,
    }


REFUSAL_NOTE = (
    "This is not a judgement about the work's value or its readiness — it is a statement about\n"
    "  WHERE it can run. Claim it LOCALLY and it proceeds untouched; only `--venue cloud` is\n"
    "  refused. If the classification is wrong, the spelling that fired is named above: fix\n"
    "  bin/cc-eligible's list rather than forcing past it, so the next item of this shape is\n"
    "  classified correctly too."
)

DEEP_HISTORY_NOTE = (
    "The sha(s) named above RESOLVE in this clone and lie outside the newest commits a cloud VM's\n"
    "  shallow clone carries, so an off-box `git show`/`log -S`/`blame` on them answers from a\n"
    "  single boundary commit — completely, confidently and WRONG\n"
    "  (docs/research/cloud-vm-shallow-clone-blast-radius-2026-08-11.md §3 S1). There is no\n"
    "  spelling list to correct here: claim it LOCALLY, or INLINE what the item needs from that\n"
    "  history into the brief so the worker never has to walk for it."
)


def cmd_check(item_id):
    idx = build_index(load_records())
    verdict, desc, hits, title = assess(item_id, idx)
    # LINE 1 is the machine-readable token: cc-backlog's caller sees only a head-biased excerpt
    # (head -1, 200 chars), so the verdict must ride the first line or it does not exist
    # (memory: claimed-outcome-vs-checked-outcome).
    sys.stdout.write("verdict=%s\n" % verdict)
    # FLUSHED BEFORE THE REASONS, and this is not tidiness. Every caller captures `2>&1`, and a
    # piped stdout is block-buffered while stderr is not — so without this the reasons overtake the
    # token and "line 1" becomes a sentence, exactly where a `head -1` consumer is looking.
    sys.stdout.flush()
    if verdict in BLOCKING:
        sys.stderr.write("OFF-BOX INELIGIBLE — %s\n" % desc)
        sys.stderr.write("  item : %s %s\n" % (item_id, title[:120]))
        sys.stderr.write("  named: %s\n" % ", ".join(hits))
        # The MEASURED class gets its own note. The spelling note ends by telling the reader to fix
        # the list, and there is no list here — sending them to edit an array over a git
        # measurement is an instruction that cannot be carried out, which is how a correct refusal
        # gets forced past instead of understood (memory: work-item-citation-refutes-its-own-remedy).
        sys.stderr.write("  " + (DEEP_HISTORY_NOTE if verdict == DEEP_HISTORY else REFUSAL_NOTE) + "\n")
    elif verdict == UNKNOWN_ITEM:
        sys.stderr.write("cc-eligible: %s — fail-open\n" % desc)
    sys.exit(3 if verdict in BLOCKING else 0)


def cmd_explain(item_id):
    idx = build_index(load_records())
    verdict, desc, hits, title = assess(item_id, idx)
    w = sys.stdout.write
    w("%s  %s\n" % (item_id, title[:120]))
    w("  verdict : %s\n" % verdict)
    w("  because : %s\n" % desc)
    w("  named   : %s\n" % (", ".join(hits) if hits else "(nothing — no spelling in the list fired)"))
    if verdict == "eligible":
        w("  ⚠️ 'eligible' means NO SPELLING IN THE LIST FIRED, which is weaker than 'this is\n"
          "     repo-only work'. The list enumerates spellings, not the class. Read the title.\n")
    sys.exit(0)


REACH_NOTE = {
    "elsewhere": "the item's project is NOT the one repo the VM is given",
    "reachable-or-unmeasured": "either the lane's own repo, or nothing measurable — arm fails OPEN",
    "not-measured": "not measured",
}

HISTORY_NOTE = {
    "ok": "certified against a FULL clone — the horizon is real",
    "no-repo": "NOT CERTIFIED: no readable git repo for this project — reach is unknown",
    "shallow": "NOT CERTIFIED: the measuring clone is ITSELF shallow, so its horizon is its whole "
               "visible history and every sha would read reachable — it cannot certify",
    "no-horizon": "NOT CERTIFIED: repo present but no trunk ref resolves — reach is unknown",
    "not-measured": "not measured",
}


def cmd_why(item_id, as_json):
    """The FULL assessment. Always exit 0 — this verb reports, it never gates.

    Separate from `check` because the two have different consumers and therefore different shapes:
    `check` speaks to a `head -1` reader inside a claim and must say ONE thing; `cc-venue` has to
    record a why that a human can read six weeks later and a `jq` can parse today.
    """
    full = assess_full(item_id, build_index(load_records()))
    if as_json:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(full, indent=2) + "\n")
        sys.exit(0)
    w = sys.stdout.write
    w("%s  %s\n" % (full["id"], full["title"][:120]))
    w("  verdict : %s\n" % full["verdict"])
    w("  project : %s\n" % (full["project"] or "(none)"))
    if full["classes"]:
        for c in full["classes"]:
            w("  refused : %-27s %s\n" % (c["verdict"], ", ".join(c["tokens"])))
    else:
        w("  refused : (nothing fired)\n")
    r = full.get("reach") or {}
    w("  reach   : %s — %s\n" % (r.get("state", "not-measured"),
                                 r.get("why") or REACH_NOTE.get(r.get("state", ""), "")))
    w("            lane=%s\n" % (r.get("lane") or "(unresolved — arm off)"))
    h = full["history"]
    w("  history : %s — %s\n" % (h["state"], HISTORY_NOTE.get(h["state"], "")))
    w("            repo=%s ref=%s depth=%s\n" % (h["repo"] or "(none)", h["ref"] or "-", h["depth"]))
    for u in h["unreachable"]:
        w("            %s — %s\n" % (u["sha"], u["why"]))
    sys.exit(0)


def _folded_items():
    """The FOLD comes from cc-backlog itself — never re-implemented here (one arbiter per fact)."""
    exe = os.environ.get("CC_ELIGIBLE_BACKLOG_BIN") or os.path.join(
        os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__)), "cc-backlog"
    )
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            [exe, "list", "--all", "--json"],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
            timeout=120,
        )
        if p.returncode != 0:
            return None
        return json.loads(p.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError, ValueError):
        return None


def cmd_sweep(as_json):
    """The census — and, more importantly, the instrument for finding the spellings this file misses.

    The ELIGIBLE bucket is printed in full and the refused ones are summarised, which is the
    opposite of how a scoreboard would be built. That is deliberate: a refusal is self-announcing
    (the token names the word that fired and a human sees it at claim time), while a WRONG eligible
    is silent by construction — it looks exactly like a right one until a VM worker improvises
    against state it cannot see. The only way to find those is to read the titles.
    """
    items = _folded_items()
    if items is None:
        _open_unknown(UNKNOWN_STORE, "cc-backlog list failed — cannot sweep")
    idx = build_index(load_records())
    nd = [i for i in items if i.get("status") != "done"]

    # PRE-WARM the history oracles: one `cat-file --batch-check` per repo for the WHOLE store,
    # instead of one per item. Without it a 485-item census is 485 subprocesses on a hot path that
    # a desk report runs routinely; with it the process count is the number of distinct projects.
    by_project = defaultdict(list)
    for it in nd:
        ent = idx.get(it["id"])
        if ent:
            by_project[ent.get("project", "")].append(ent["text"])
    for proj, texts in by_project.items():
        orc = oracle_for(proj)
        if orc.certify() == "ok":
            toks = set()
            for t in texts:
                toks.update(HEX.findall(t))
            orc.resolve_many(toks)

    buckets = defaultdict(list)
    tokens = Counter()
    horizons = {}
    for it in nd:
        full = assess_full(it["id"], idx)
        buckets[full["verdict"]].append({
            "id": it["id"], "status": it.get("status"),
            "title": (it.get("title") or "")[:120], "tokens": full["tokens"],
        })
        # SPELLINGS ONLY. The measured class's "tokens" are cited SHAs, and folding those into this
        # counter would put a few dozen one-off hex strings alongside `pane×100` and — worse — make
        # the "never fired" line below (which is the instrument for finding a WRONG word) read
        # against a vocabulary that is not a vocabulary.
        if full["verdict"] != DEEP_HISTORY:
            tokens.update(full["tokens"])
        h = full["history"]
        if h["repo"]:
            horizons[h["repo"]] = {"state": h["state"], "ref": h["ref"], "depth": h["depth"]}

    report = {
        "non_done": len(nd),
        "store": BACKLOG,
        "counts": {v: len(buckets.get(v, [])) for v in
                   list(BLOCKING) + ["eligible", UNKNOWN_ITEM, UNKNOWN_STORE]},
        "tokens": dict(tokens.most_common()),
        # The certification, per repo. A census whose deep-history bucket reads 0 means one of two
        # opposite things — nothing cites old history, or nothing could be measured — and only this
        # block tells them apart (memory: sensor-default-off-makes-blindness-the-shipping-path).
        "horizons": horizons,
        "eligible": buckets.get("eligible", []),
        "ineligible": {v: buckets.get(v, []) for v in BLOCKING},
    }
    if as_json:
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(report, indent=2) + "\n")
        sys.exit(0)

    w = sys.stdout.write
    w("cc-eligible sweep — %d non-done items · store %s\n" % (len(nd), BACKLOG))
    for repo, h in sorted(horizons.items()):
        w("  horizon: %-52s %s (ref=%s depth=%s)\n"
          % (repo, h["state"], h["ref"] or "-", h["depth"]))
    w("\n  REFUSED off-box (exit 3 at `cc-backlog claim --venue cloud`):\n")
    for v, desc in SWEEP_ROWS:
        w("    %-27s %-58s : %d\n" % (v, desc[:58], len(buckets.get(v, []))))
    w("  ALLOWED off-box:\n")
    w("    %-27s %-58s : %d\n"
      % ("eligible", "no spelling fired — NOT a proof of repo-only", len(buckets.get("eligible", []))))
    unk = len(buckets.get(UNKNOWN_ITEM, []))
    if unk:
        w("    %-27s %-58s : %d\n" % (UNKNOWN_ITEM, "folded but not in the raw ledger", unk))
    if tokens:
        w("\n  spellings that fired: %s\n"
          % ", ".join("%s×%d" % (t, n) for t, n in tokens.most_common()))
        never = [t for _v, _d, pats in CLASSES for t, _rx in pats if t not in tokens]
        if never:
            w("  never fired (a dead spelling is not a clean store — it may be the wrong word): %s\n"
              % ", ".join(never))
    for v, _desc in SWEEP_ROWS:
        rows = buckets.get(v, [])
        if not rows:
            continue
        w("\n── %s ─────────────────────────────────\n" % v.upper())
        for r in rows:
            w("  %s [%s] %s\n" % (r["id"], r["status"], r["title"]))
            w("      named: %s\n" % ", ".join(r["tokens"]))
    w("\n── ELIGIBLE — READ THESE ────────────────────────────────────\n")
    w("  This is the bucket where a missed spelling does damage: a wrong refusal announces itself,\n"
      "  a wrong ELIGIBLE puts a worker in a VM against state it cannot see. If a title below\n"
      "  names local-only work, the spelling it uses is missing from bin/cc-eligible — add it.\n")
    for r in buckets.get("eligible", []):
        w("  %s [%s] %s\n" % (r["id"], r["status"], r["title"]))
    sys.exit(0)


def main():
    argv = sys.argv[1:]
    if not argv or argv[0] in ("-h", "--help", "help"):
        sys.stdout.write(__doc__)
        sys.exit(0)
    verb, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    if verb in ("check", "explain", "why"):
        if not rest:
            sys.stderr.write("cc-eligible %s: <id> is required\n" % verb)
            sys.exit(2)
        if verb == "why":
            cmd_why(rest[0], "--json" in rest)
        else:
            (cmd_check if verb == "check" else cmd_explain)(rest[0])
    elif verb == "sweep":
        cmd_sweep("--json" in rest)
    else:
        sys.stderr.write("cc-eligible: unknown verb %s\n" % verb)
        sys.exit(2)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        main()
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 — the whole point is that NOTHING here may block a claim
        _open_unknown(UNKNOWN_STORE, "unexpected error (%s) — fail-open" % exc)
